/* Paper III, Part B — General Forensic. Topics IV-VI. */

window.MPSC.units.push({
  id: 'p3gf4',
  paper: 'Paper III — General Forensic',
  title: 'IV. Questioned Documents',
  marks: 10,
  syllabus: 'Definition and importance of Questioned documents. Forgery and its types & characteristics. Invisible and indented writing. Importance of handwriting examination in forensics. Identifying features of fake and genuine Indian currency notes. Different Types of alterations - erasures, additions, overwritings and obliterations. Difference between photomicrography & microphotography.',

  notes: [
    { h: 'Questioned Documents, Handwriting & Forgery Types',
      b: '<p>A <b>Questioned Document (QD)</b> is any material bearing marks, symbols or writing whose authenticity, origin, age or alteration is in dispute (wills, cheques, suicide notes, ransom notes, passports, currency, contracts, painted graffiti). Pioneer of modern Questioned Document examination: <b>Albert S. Osborn</b> (author of <em>Questioned Documents</em>, 1910). In India, the Government Examiner of Questioned Documents (GEQD) was first established at <b>Shimla in 1906</b>.</p>' +
         '<div class="analogy"><b>💡 Analogy — Genuine Signature vs Simulated Forgery:</b> Signing your own name is like <b>riding a bicycle at full speed down a familiar street</b> — it is a subconscious, automatic neuromuscular reflex (ballistic movement) with smooth curves and tapered "flying starts and flying finishes" as the pen hits and leaves the paper in motion. Forging someone else\'s signature by copying is like <b>slowly walking across a high tightrope while staring at a map</b> — because the forger is drawing rather than writing, the line slows down, wobbles with fine <b>tremor</b>, shows <b>blunt starts and stops</b> (where the pen sat still on the paper), and contains hidden <b>pen lifts and retouching (patching)</b>.</div>' +
         '<table><tr><th>Forgery Type</th><th>Method</th><th>Tell-Tale Microscopic Signs</th></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>Freehand / Simulated</b></td><td>Copying a genuine model signature by eye</td><td>Slow drawn movement, <b>line tremor of fraud</b>, blunt entry/exit strokes, hesitation pen lifts, careful retouching/patching, uniform heavy pressure.</td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>Traced Forgery</b></td><td>Tracing over a genuine model via transmitted light (window/lightbox), carbon paper, or indented guide line</td><td>Carbon/graphite particles or uninked indentation furrow underneath the ink line, slow wavering strokes, and <b>near-exact geometric superimposition</b> with the model signature.</td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>Simple / Spurious (Disguised)</b></td><td>Signing another person\'s name in the forger\'s own handwriting (no model available)</td><td>Fluent fast speed (no tremor!), but zero resemblance to the victim\'s signature; reveals the <b>forger\'s own unconscious handwriting habits</b>.</td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>Guided-Hand / Assisted</b></td><td>Infirm/elderly writer\'s hand is physically held and guided by another person</td><td>Conflict of impulses, erratic alignment, abnormal letter forms combining features of two writers (unlike genuine <em>tremor of age/illness</em>, which shows jerky involuntary deviations even on airborne pen approaches).</td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>Transplanted / Cut-and-Paste</b></td><td>Digitally scanning or physically lifting a genuine signature onto a fraudulent document</td><td><b>100% identical pixel-for-pixel superimposition</b> with the source signature, toner/ink-jet halftone dots instead of continuous wet ink furrow.</td></tr></table>' +
         '<div class="vs-grid">' +
         '<div class="vs-card"><h4>📘 Class Characteristics of Handwriting</h4><p>Features taught to an entire group of learners by a school <b>copybook system</b> (e.g., Palmer, Spencerian, Zaner-Bloser, Marion Richardson) or regional script:</p><ul>' +
         '<li>General style (cursive vs block/print vs mixed)</li>' +
         '<li>Standard copybook letter shapes and baseline orientation</li>' +
         '<li>Broadly narrows down educational background or geographical origin, never identifies an individual.</li></ul></div>' +
         '<div class="vs-card"><h4>🖋️ Individual Characteristics of Handwriting</h4><p>Subconscious personal habits and neuromuscular divergences from the copybook norm that uniquely identify a writer (when present in sufficient combination):</p><ul>' +
         '<li><b>Line quality, speed, rhythm &amp; pen pressure shading</b></li>' +
         '<li><b>Slant / slope</b>, <b>relative height ratios</b> (upper, middle, lower zones)</li>' +
         '<li><b>Connecting strokes</b> (garland, arcade, angular, thread), initial/terminal spurs</li>' +
         '<li>Exact placement &amp; shape of <b>i-dots, t-crossings</b>, loops, diacritics, and baseline alignment.</li></ul></div>' +
         '</div>' +
         '<div class="clarity"><b>⚡ Concept Clarity — Natural Variation vs Identical Superimposition:</b> No human being is a printing press: every time you sign your name, there is slight <b>natural variation</b> within your personal master pattern. Therefore, if two signatures on two cheques <b>superimpose 100% identically</b> when held up to the light, at least one of them is <b>definitely a forgery</b> (traced or digitally copied/transplanted)!</div>' },

    { h: 'Document Alterations, Indented & Invisible Writing',
      b: '<ul><li><b>Erasures</b> — removal of writing from paper:<ul>' +
         '<li><b>Mechanical (Abrasive) Erasure:</b> Using a rubber eraser, razor blade, knife or sandpaper. Disturbs and raises surface paper fibres, destroys surface sizing/gloss, and thins the paper. Detected by <b>low-angle oblique light, transmitted light, side-lighting microscopy, or iodine fuming</b> (which stains disturbed fibres brown).</li>' +
         '<li><b>Chemical Erasure (Bleaching):</b> Using oxidising/reducing agents (sodium hypochlorite, oxalic acid, potassium permanganate, "ink eradicators") to decolourise ink dye without removing it. Paper fibres remain smooth, but chemical residues cause a tell-tale <b>patch of fluorescence or quenching under Ultraviolet (UV) light</b> and yellow discoloration over time.</li></ul></li>' +
         '<li><b>Obliteration (Masking)</b> — smearing heavy ink, black marker or white correction fluid (whitener) over original writing to make it unreadable. Deciphered non-destructively using <b>Infrared (IR) Reflectance or Infrared Luminescence</b> in a <b>VSC (Video Spectral Comparator)</b>: if the masking ink is transparent to IR (~700–1000 nm) while the underlying ink absorbs IR (like carbon/iron-gall ink), the top layer vanishes on the screen and the hidden writing reads clearly! (Organic solvents like toluene/chloroform or transmitted light also reveal correction-fluid obliterations.)</li>' +
         '<li><b>Addition (Interlineation / Insertion)</b> — squeezing extra words, digits (e.g., turning "100" into "1000" or "Nine" into "Ninety") or whole lines into an existing document after execution. Detected by cramped spacing, baseline shift, different ink luminescence/spectra under VSC (IR/UV), <b>TLC / HPLC ink dye profiling</b>, and <b>intersecting stroke sequence</b> (determining whether the text stroke or fold/signature stroke was made last).</li>' +
         '<li><b>Overwriting (Retouching)</b> — writing directly on top of existing characters to alter a figure (e.g., "3" → "8", "1" → "4") or patch a weak forged stroke. Exposed by double tracks under stereomicroscopy and differing ink behaviour under filtered IR/UV light.</li></ul>' +
         '<p><b>Indented Writing</b> — invisible pressure furrows embossed into underlying sheets of a notepad when someone writes on the top sheet. Developed non-destructively by:</p>' +
         '<ol><li><b>Oblique (grazing) lighting</b> at $5^\\circ\\text{–}15^\\circ$ angle in a darkened room (shadows fill the indentations).</li>' +
         '<li><b>ESDA (Electrostatic Detection Apparatus)</b> — invented by Foster &amp; Freeman; pulls the document onto a porous brass vacuum platen, lays a thin transparent <b>Mylar (polymer) film</b> over it, passes a high-voltage <b>corona discharge wire</b> across the film (creating differential electrostatic charge over indented paper fibres), and cascades negatively charged black toner beads across the film. Preserved permanently under adhesive laminating film. Because ESDA is <b>100% non-destructive</b> and ruined by humidity/wetting, <b>ESDA must ALWAYS be performed BEFORE ninhydrin/DFO fingerprint processing!</b></li></ol>' +
         '<p><b>Invisible (Secret / Sympathetic) Writing:</b> Messages written with colourless fluids: (a) <b>Organic fluids</b> (lemon juice, milk, onion juice, urine, vinegar, sugar solution) → turn brown on <b>gentle heating</b> (caramelisation/weak acid charring of cellulose) or fluoresce under <b>UV light</b>; (b) <b>Starch solution</b> → turns deep blue with weak <b>iodine vapour/solution</b>; (c) <b>Phenolphthalein</b> → turns pink with <b>alkali/ammonia vapour</b>; (d) <b>Cobalt chloride</b> → turns blue on warming.</p>' },

    { h: 'Genuine vs Fake Indian Currency Notes (FICN)',
      b: '<p>Genuine Reserve Bank of India banknotes (<b>Mahatma Gandhi New Series</b>) incorporate multi-level overt and covert security features:</p>' +
         '<ul><li><b>Paper Substrate:</b> 100% pure <b>cotton rag paper</b> (no wood pulp) treated with polyvinyl alcohol for crispness ("crackling" sound) — appears <b>dark/dull under UV light</b> (contains no optical brighteners, whereas ordinary photocopier paper glows bright blue-white under UV!).</li>' +
         '<li><b>True Watermark &amp; Electrotype Watermark:</b> Mahatma Gandhi portrait and denomination numeral created <b>during paper manufacture by varying pulp fibre density/thickness</b> on a dandy roll. Visible only in <b>transmitted light</b> (Counterfeiters print fake watermarks using white/oily ink, which remain visible even in reflected light).</li>' +
         '<li><b>Windowed Colour-Shifting Security Thread:</b> Machine-readable thread woven into the paper (appears as dashes on the front, continuous line on the back in transmitted light), bearing micro-inscriptions <em>भारत</em> and <em>RBI</em>; shifts from <b>green to blue</b> when tilted on higher denominations.</li>' +
         '<li><b>Intaglio (Raised) Printing:</b> Portrait of Mahatma Gandhi, RBI seal, Ashoka Pillar emblem, guarantee clause, and <b>bleed lines / tactile angular identification marks for the visually impaired</b> (on left/right margins) are printed by high-pressure engraved steel plates so the ink sits noticeably raised to the touch.</li>' +
         '<li><b>See-Through Register:</b> Floral/numeral design printed half on the front and half on the back in hollow outlines that align with sub-millimetre precision into a complete numeral when held against light.</li>' +
         '<li><b>Latent Image:</b> Hidden denomination numeral inside the vertical/horizontal band that appears only when the note is held flat at eye level toward a light source.</li>' +
         '<li><b>Optically Variable Ink (OVI):</b> Denomination numeral printed in colour-shifting ink (green to blue on tilting).</li>' +
         '<li><b>Micro-lettering</b> (readable only with a magnifying glass between the portrait and vertical band) &amp; <b>Dual-fluorescent Number Panels + Optical Fibres</b> glowing under UV.</li></ul>' +
         '<p>Counterfeiting currency notes (FICN) is punishable under <b>Sections 178–182 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS), 2023</b> (formerly IPC Sections 489A–489E).</p>' },

    { h: 'Photomicrography vs Microphotography',
      b: '<p>A classic verbatim syllabus distinction that tests precision of terminology:</p>' +
         '<div class="vs-grid">' +
         '<div class="vs-card"><h4>🔬 Photomicrography (Forensic Lab Standard)</h4><p><b>Photographing a microscopic object through a compound/stereo/comparison microscope</b> by attaching a camera to the ocular tube to produce a <b>highly ENLARGED image</b>.<br><b>Forensic Uses:</b> Recording bullet striations, toolmarks, ink line crossovers, hair medullae, spermatozoa, and fingerprint poroscopy for presentation to the court.</p></div>' +
         '<div class="vs-card"><h4>🎞️ Microphotography (Espionage / Archival)</h4><p><b>Optically reducing a large document or scene onto a tiny negative</b> (such as <b>microfilm, microfiche, or a spy "microdot"</b> the size of a full stop) that can only be read back using a magnifier.<br><b>Mnemonic:</b> Photo<b>micro</b>graphy photographs a <em>micro-object</em> (makes tiny things BIG); <b>Micro</b>photography makes a <em>micro-photograph</em> (makes big documents TINY).</p></div>' +
         '</div>' }
  ],

  questions: [
    { q: 'Two signatures that superimpose almost exactly on one another indicate', o: ['Both are genuine', 'Tracing or mechanical reproduction', 'A guided hand signature', 'Normal natural variation'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>No person signs twice identically — <b>natural variation</b> is an inherent feature of genuine writing. Near-exact superimposition therefore indicates <b>tracing</b> or a mechanical/scanned reproduction.</p><p>This inverts the intuitive expectation, which is exactly why it is examined: candidates assume similarity proves genuineness.</p>' },

    { q: 'ESDA (Electrostatic Detection Apparatus) is used to detect', o: ['Erased ink', 'Indented writing impressions', 'Counterfeit watermarks', 'Ink age'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>ESDA develops <b>indented impressions</b> left when writing on a sheet above, and can recover them from several sheets down.</p><p>Its major advantage is being <b>non-destructive</b> — the document is unaltered and remains available for other examinations, so ESDA is performed <em>before</em> any destructive testing.</p>' },

    { q: 'Obliterated writing covered with a different ink is most effectively read using', o: ['Ultraviolet fluorescence only', 'Infrared examination', 'X-ray diffraction', 'Polarised light microscopy'], a: 1,
      e: '<p><b>IR examination</b> works because inks that look identical in visible light frequently differ in IR absorption or luminescence — the covering ink becomes transparent while the underlying ink remains visible.</p><p>UV is more useful for chemical erasures and paper treatment. IR is the first-line technique for obliteration specifically.</p>' },

    { q: 'A forged signature produced by freehand simulation typically shows', o: ['Rapid, fluent line quality', 'Tremor, blunt beginnings and endings, and pen lifts', 'Greater natural variation than the genuine', 'Heavier pressure throughout'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>The forger writes <b>slowly and consciously</b>, producing tremor, hesitation, blunt starts and stops where the pen was placed and lifted deliberately, and unnatural pen lifts mid-stroke.</p><p>Genuine writing is fast and automatic, giving tapered strokes and fluent line quality. The underlying principle — <em>speed betrays authenticity</em> — resolves most questions in this topic.</p>' },

    { q: 'The watermark on a genuine Indian banknote is produced by', o: ['Printing with fluorescent ink', 'Varying the thickness of the paper during manufacture', 'Embossing after printing', 'Applying a transparent film'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>A true watermark is formed in the <b>paper itself</b> by varying its thickness while still wet, so it is visible only in <b>transmitted</b> light and cannot be felt.</p><p>Counterfeits <em>print</em> a simulated watermark, which is consequently visible in reflected light too — the standard field test is simply to look at the note without backlighting.</p>' },

    { q: 'Photomicrography is best defined as', o: ['Producing greatly reduced photographs such as microfilm', 'Photographing a small object through a microscope to obtain an enlarged image', 'Photographing documents under UV light', 'Photographing a crime scene from a distance'], a: 1,
      e: '<p><b>Photomicrography</b> = photography <em>through a microscope</em>, giving an enlarged image — the routine laboratory technique for recording striations, fibres and ridge detail.</p><p>Option (a) is <b>microphotography</b>, the reverse operation, and is the intended distractor. The syllabus names both together precisely so they can be contrasted.</p>' },

    { q: 'A chemical erasure on a document is best revealed by', o: ['Infrared luminescence', 'Ultraviolet examination showing a stain or altered fluorescence', 'ESDA', 'Comparison microscopy'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>Bleaching agents leave residues that alter the paper\'s <b>UV fluorescence</b>, so the treated area appears as a differently fluorescing patch.</p><p><em>Mechanical</em> erasure is better shown by oblique light, which reveals disturbed fibres and thinned paper. Distinguishing the two erasure types by the technique used to detect them is the usual framing.</p>' },

    { q: 'A ransom note requires both ESDA examination for indented writing and Ninhydrin processing for latent fingerprints. In what order MUST these two examinations be performed?', o: ['Ninhydrin first, then ESDA', 'ESDA first, then Ninhydrin', 'Simultaneously in the same chamber', 'Either order produces identical results'], a: 1,
      e: '<p><b>ESDA must always be performed BEFORE Ninhydrin (or any wet chemical treatment)</b>. Exposure to high humidity or liquid solvents relaxes the compressed paper fibres and destroys the electrostatic charge differential needed for ESDA, whereas ESDA itself is completely dry and non-destructive.</p>' },

    { q: 'A forgery in which the perpetrator makes no attempt to copy the victim\'s genuine signature (often because they have no model to copy) and simply signs the victim\'s name in their own natural or slightly disguised handwriting is called a', o: ['Simulated forgery', 'Traced forgery', 'Simple (spurious) forgery', 'Transplanted forgery'], a: 2,
      e: '<p>A <b>simple (or spurious) forgery</b> is written without a model of the genuine signature. Unlike simulated or traced forgeries, it displays <b>fast, natural line quality without tremor</b>, which allows the document examiner to positively identify the <b>forger</b> from their own unconscious handwriting characteristics!</p>' },

    { q: 'The tactile raised feeling of the Mahatma Gandhi portrait, RBI seal and angular bleed lines for the visually impaired on a genuine Indian currency note is produced by', o: ['Offset lithography', 'Intaglio (engraved steel plate) printing', 'Laser toner fusion', 'Screen printing'], a: 1,
      e: '<p><b>Intaglio printing</b> uses high pressure to force viscous ink from recessed grooves of an engraved plate onto the cotton rag paper, creating embossed, raised ink ridges that can be felt with the fingertips. Most counterfeit notes made on inkjet or offset presses feel completely flat and smooth.</p>' },

    { q: 'When a genuine Indian banknote and a counterfeit note printed on ordinary commercial bond/photocopier paper are placed side by side under Ultraviolet (UV) light, how does the paper substrate itself behave?', o: ['Genuine banknote paper glows bright blue-white; commercial paper stays dark', 'Genuine 100% cotton rag banknote paper stays dull/dark (only its security fibres and number panels fluoresce), whereas commercial paper glows bright blue-white due to optical brighteners', 'Both glow bright green', 'UV light destroys genuine banknote paper'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>Genuine currency paper is made from <b>pure cotton rag free of optical brightening agents (OBAs)</b>, so the background paper appears <b>dark/non-fluorescent under UV</b> (putting the glowing fluorescent security fibres and serial numbers in sharp contrast). Ordinary commercial printing paper is loaded with OBAs and glows brightly all over under UV.</p>' },

    { q: 'Which laboratory instrument combines visible, Ultraviolet, Infrared reflectance, Infrared luminescence, coaxial and oblique lighting with digital filters to examine ink differentiation, obliterations and security documents non-destructively?', o: ['Gas Chromatograph (GC-MS)', 'Video Spectral Comparator (VSC)', 'Atomic Absorption Spectrometer (AAS)', 'Thermal Cycler'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>The <b>Video Spectral Comparator (VSC)</b> is the workhorse instrument of the Questioned Documents laboratory. By illuminating a document at selected wavelengths from UV (254/365 nm) through visible to near-IR (700–1000 nm) and viewing through long-pass cut-off filters, it reveals obliterations, additions in different inks, chemical erasures and passport/currency security features without damaging the document.</p>' },

    { q: 'Where and in what year was the institution of the Government Examiner of Questioned Documents (GEQD) first established in India?', o: ['Calcutta in 1897', 'Shimla in 1906', 'Hyderabad in 1968', 'New Delhi in 1972'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>The first <b>GEQD (Government Examiner of Questioned Documents)</b> in India was established by the British Government at <b>Shimla in 1906</b> (headed by C.R. Hardless) to examine writings related to the Indian independence movement. (Calcutta in 1897 is the world\'s first <em>Fingerprint Bureau</em> — a classic exam contrast!)</p>' },

    { q: 'To decipher an indented writing impression on a sheet of paper at a crime scene when an ESDA machine is not immediately available, the simplest non-destructive optical technique is', o: ['Direct vertical high-intensity lighting', 'Low-angle oblique (grazing) lighting in a darkened room', 'Immersion in silver nitrate', 'Heating over an open flame'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>Shining a light beam nearly parallel to the paper surface (<b>oblique / grazing illumination at $5^\\circ\\text{–}15^\\circ$</b>) casts dark shadows inside the depressed troughs of the indented strokes while illuminating the raised ridges, making the colourless impression legible for photography.</p>' }
  ]
});

window.MPSC.units.push({
  id: 'p3gf5',
  paper: 'Paper III — General Forensic',
  title: 'V. Forensic DNA / Biology',
  marks: 10,
  syllabus: 'Cell- structure and cell organelles. Structure of DNA and RNA. Hereditary, Alleles & mutations. Mendel\'s law of inheritance. Concept of genetics polymorphism. Antigen and Antibody. Human skeletal system. Forensic significance of Blood, Semen, & Hair. Blood composition and blood groups (ABO, Rh, MN system), Secretor and non-secretor. Morphology of spermatozoa.',

  notes: [
    { h: 'Cell Organelles, DNA/RNA Structure & Genetics',
      b: '<p><b>Eukaryotic Cell Organelles &amp; Forensic Relevance:</b></p>' +
         '<ul><li><b>Nucleus</b> — contains <b>23 pairs (46) of linear chromosomes</b> (22 pairs of autosomes + 1 pair of sex chromosomes: XX female, XY male). Source of highly discriminating <b>nuclear DNA (nDNA)</b> (~3.2 billion base pairs; 1 diploid copy per cell). Absent in mature mammalian Red Blood Cells (RBCs) and keratinised hair shafts!</li>' +
         '<li><b>Mitochondria</b> — the "powerhouse of the cell" (ATP synthesis via oxidative phosphorylation). Contains its own circular <b>mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA)</b> (~16,569 bp, hypervariable control regions HV1 &amp; HV2), inherited <b>strictly from the mother</b> (sperm midpiece mitochondria are destroyed after fertilisation). Hundreds to thousands of copies per cell.</li>' +
         '<li><b>Ribosomes</b> (protein translation), <b>Endoplasmic Reticulum &amp; Golgi apparatus</b> (protein folding/secretion), <b>Lysosomes</b> (hydrolytic enzymes causing post-mortem autolysis).</li></ul>' +
         '<p><b>Structure of DNA &amp; RNA:</b> DNA is a right-handed <b>antiparallel double helix</b> ($5\'\\to 3\'$ and $3\'\\to 5\'$, Watson &amp; Crick, 1953; X-ray diffraction by Rosalind Franklin). Nucleotide = <b>2\'-deoxyribose sugar + phosphate group + nitrogenous base</b>. Purines (2 rings): <b>Adenine (A), Guanine (G)</b>; Pyrimidines (1 ring): <b>Cytosine (C), Thymine (T)</b>. Base pairing: <b>A=T (2 hydrogen bonds)</b> and <b>G≡C (3 hydrogen bonds</b> — making GC-rich DNA have a higher melting temperature $T_m$). <b>RNA</b> is single-stranded, uses <b>ribose sugar</b> (has a $2\'$-OH group making it less stable than DNA), and substitutes <b>Uracil (U)</b> for Thymine.</p>' +
         '<div class="vs-grid">' +
         '<div class="vs-card"><h4>🧬 Autosomal Nuclear STR DNA</h4><ul>' +
         '<li><b>Location &amp; Copy No.:</b> Cell nucleus; only <b>2 copies (diploid)</b> per nucleated cell (1 maternal, 1 paternal).</li>' +
         '<li><b>Forensic Power:</b> <b>Individualises to a single person</b> (except identical twins). Amplifies <b>Short Tandem Repeats (STRs)</b> (2–6 bp core repeats, e.g., CODIS 20 core loci + <b>Amelogenin</b> for sexing: XX gives single 106 bp peak; XY gives 106 bp &amp; 112 bp peaks).</li>' +
         '<li><b>Best Samples:</b> Fresh blood (WBCs), semen (sperm heads), buccal swabs, hair <em>with follicular root sheath (anagen)</em>.</li></ul></div>' +
         '<div class="vs-card"><h4>🔋 Mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) &amp; Y-STRs</h4><ul>' +
         '<li><b>mtDNA:</b> Circular, 100s–1,000s of copies per cell → survives in <b>severely degraded/charred remains, ancient bones, teeth, and shed telogen hair shafts WITHOUT roots</b>. <b>Maternally inherited</b> (shared by mother, all siblings, and maternal grandmother → <em>cannot individualise</em>).</li>' +
         '<li><b>Y-STRs (on Y chromosome):</b> <b>Paternally inherited</b> (shared by father, sons, paternal uncles). Crucial in <b>gang-rape or male-female sexual assault mixtures</b> (even with vasectomised/azoospermic males) to isolate the minor male profile against massive female epithelial background.</li></ul></div>' +
         '</div>' +
         '<p><b>Mendel\'s Laws &amp; Genetic Polymorphism:</b></p>' +
         '<ul><li><b>Alleles:</b> Alternative forms of a gene/locus at the same chromosomal position (homozygous = two identical alleles, e.g., 12,12; heterozygous = two different alleles, e.g., 12,15).</li>' +
         '<li><b>Law of Segregation (1st Law):</b> Two alleles for a trait separate during gamete formation (meiosis) so each sperm or egg carries only one allele.</li>' +
         '<li><b>Law of Independent Assortment (2nd Law):</b> Alleles of genes on different chromosomes assort independently of one another — allowing forensic geneticists to multiply locus probabilities together (the <b>Product Rule</b>) under Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium ($p^2 + 2pq + q^2 = 1$).</li>' +
         '<li><b>Polymorphism:</b> Occurrence of multiple alleles at a locus in a population (length polymorphism in VNTRs/STRs vs sequence polymorphism in SNPs and mtDNA). Blood groups illustrate <b>co-dominance</b> ($I^A$ and $I^B$ are both expressed in group AB; $M$ and $N$ are co-dominant in MN blood group) and <b>recessiveness</b> ($i$ in group O).</li></ul>' },

    { h: 'Blood — Composition, 4-Step Identification Pipeline & Blood Grouping',
      b: '<p><b>Blood Composition:</b> Liquid <b>plasma (~55%</b> — 90% water + albumin, globulins/antibodies, clotting fibrinogen; <em>serum</em> is plasma minus fibrinogen/clotting factors) and <b>formed cellular elements (~45%</b> — Erythrocytes/RBCs carry haemoglobin; Leucocytes/WBCs provide immunity; Thrombocytes/platelets clot blood).</p>' +
         '<div class="analogy"><b>💡 Analogy — Why Blood DNA Comes from White Blood Cells, Not Red Blood Cells:</b> Think of a mature human <b>Red Blood Cell (RBC) as a cargo delivery van that ripped out its driver\'s cabin (the cell nucleus)</b> so it could pack in 100% more oxygen tanks (haemoglobin). Because human RBCs jettison their nucleus and mitochondria as they mature in the bone marrow, they contain <b>zero DNA</b>! <b>White Blood Cells (leucocytes)</b> are the tactical command vehicles — fewer in number (~1 WBC per 700 RBCs), but every single one keeps its full nuclear headquarters intact. That is why every forensic DNA profile extracted from a bloodstain actually comes from the <b>White Blood Cells</b>. (By contrast, non-mammalian vertebrates — birds, reptiles, amphibians and fish — have <em>nucleated oval RBCs</em>!)</div>' +
         '<div class="vs-grid">' +
         '<div class="vs-card"><h4>🧪 Step 1: Presumptive (Catalytic Colour) Blood Tests</h4><p>All rely on the <b>peroxidase-like catalytic activity of the Haem group</b> in haemoglobin, which breaks down hydrogen peroxide ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2$) to oxidise a colourless reduced dye into a coloured or glowing product. Extremely sensitive (up to $1:10^5\\text{–}10^6$), so <b>a negative test rules blood OUT, while a positive test requires confirmation</b> (false positives: horseradish/turnip plant peroxidases, rust/iron/copper salts, bleach):</p><ul>' +
         '<li><b>Kastle–Meyer (Phenolphthalein) test:</b> Reduced phenolphthalein + $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2$ → bright <b>pink</b> colour immediately.</li>' +
         '<li><b>Leucomalachite Green (LMG):</b> → <b>blue-green</b> colour.</li>' +
         '<li><b>Tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) / Hemastix:</b> → <b>blue-green</b> (replaced Benzidine &amp; Ortho-tolidine, which are potent bladder carcinogens).</li>' +
         '<li><b>Luminol (3-aminophthalhydrazide):</b> Sprayed in complete darkness → glowing <b>blue-white chemiluminescence</b> (~425 nm); reveals latent/washed bloodstains on carpets and floors without destroying DNA for subsequent STR typing.</li></ul></div>' +
         '<div class="vs-card"><h4>💎 Step 2: Confirmatory (Microcrystal) Blood Tests</h4><p>Prove the stain truly contains <b>haemoglobin derivatives</b> by forming characteristic microscopic crystals on a glass slide:</p><ul>' +
         '<li><b>Takayama Test (Haemochromogen crystal test):</b> Stain heated gently with <b>pyridine, glucose (reducing sugar) and NaOH</b> → forms salmon-pink, feathery/sheaf-like crystals of <b>pyridine ferroprotoporphyrin (haemochromogen)</b>. Preferred over Teichmann because it is less sensitive to overheating and works better on aged/rusty stains.</li>' +
         '<li><b>Teichmann Test (Haemin crystal test):</b> Stain heated with <b>glacial acetic acid and a trace of halide salt (NaCl / KBr / KI)</b> → forms dark <b>rhombic brown crystals of haemin (ferriprotoporphyrin chloride)</b>.</li>' +
         '<li><b>Spectroscopic / Immunochromatographic Confirmation:</b> Characteristic absorption bands of oxyhaemoglobin (540 &amp; 576 nm) or lateral-flow strip tests (<b>ABAcard HemaTrace</b> targeting human haemoglobin — simultaneously confirms blood AND human origin!).</li></ul></div>' +
         '</div>' +
         '<p><b>Step 3 (Species of Origin — Is it Human?):</b> Classical immunology uses the <b>Precipitin Test</b> (Uhlenhuth ring test or <b>Ouchterlony double immunodiffusion</b> in agar gel, or <b>Crossover Electrophoresis</b>): extract of the bloodstain (containing human serum proteins/antigens) is reacted against <b>Anti-Human Globulin (AHG) antiserum</b> raised in a rabbit. A white <b>precipitin band/line</b> at the interface proves the blood is <b>human</b>.</p>' +
         '<p><b>Step 4 (Individualisation / Grouping):</b> Modern STR DNA profiling, or classical serology:</p>' +
         '<table><tr><th>ABO Group</th><th>Antigen (Agglutinogen) on RBC membrane</th><th>Antibody (Isoagglutinin) in Serum</th><th>Transfusion Status</th></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>Group A</b></td><td>Antigen A</td><td>Anti-B</td><td>Receives A, O</td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>Group B</b></td><td>Antigen B</td><td>Anti-A</td><td>Receives B, O</td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>Group AB</b></td><td>Both A and B antigens</td><td><b>Neither Anti-A nor Anti-B</b></td><td><b>Universal RBC Recipient</b></td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>Group O</b></td><td>Neither A nor B (only H antigen precursor; <em>Bombay phenotype $O_h$ lacks even H antigen!</em>)</td><td>Both Anti-A and Anti-B</td><td><b>Universal RBC Donor</b></td></tr></table>' +
         '<p><b>Rh System</b> (Landsteiner &amp; Wiener, 1940, from <em>Rhesus</em> monkey; D antigen is most immunogenic; $\\text{Rh}^-$ mother carrying $\\text{Rh}^+$ foetus risks <b>erythroblastosis fetalis / haemolytic disease of the newborn</b>). <b>MN System</b> (glycophorin A on RBCs; co-dominant alleles $L^M$ and $L^N$ yield phenotypes M, N, and MN; no natural anti-M/anti-N antibodies exist in human serum).<br>' +
         '<b>Secretors (~80% of humans, carrying dominant $Se$ gene — $SeSe$ or $Sese$):</b> Secrete water-soluble ABO blood-group antigens into their saliva, semen, vaginal fluid, tears, sweat and gastric juice (detected via <b>Absorption–Inhibition</b> or <b>Absorption–Elution</b> Lattes crust techniques). <b>Non-secretors (~20%, homozygous recessive $sese$)</b> do not secrete ABO antigens into body fluids.</p>' +
         '<div class="example"><b>🔬 Worked Example —Chargaff\'s Rule Calculation + Crime Scene Stain Pipeline:</b><br>' +
         '<b>Part A (Chargaff\'s Rule):</b> A double-stranded human nuclear DNA extract from a crime scene contains <b>18% Cytosine (C)</b>. Find the percentages of G, A, and T.<br>' +
         '• By Watson–Crick pairing ($G \\equiv C$), <b>% Guanine (G) = % Cytosine (C) = 18%</b>.<br>' +
         '• Total $G + C = 18\\% + 18\\% = 36\\%$.<br>' +
         '• Remaining bases $A + T = 100\\% - 36\\% = 64\\%$.<br>' +
         '• Since $A = T$, <b>% Adenine (A) = % Thymine (T) = $64\\% \\div 2 =$ 32% each!</b><br><br>' +
         '<b>Part B (4-Step Bloodstain Pipeline):</b> Reddish-brown splatters are found on a suspect\'s boots.<br>' +
         '<code>1. Presumptive:</code> Swab turns bright pink with <b>Kastle–Meyer</b> reagent → possibly blood.<br>' +
         '<code>2. Confirmatory:</code> Pyridine + glucose + NaOH gives salmon-pink feathery <b>Takayama haemochromogen crystals</b> → confirmed blood.<br>' +
         '<code>3. Species:</code> Precipitin line with rabbit <b>anti-human serum</b> (or ABAcard HemaTrace) → confirmed <b>human</b> blood.<br>' +
         '<code>4. Individualisation:</code> DNA extracted from nucleated <b>WBCs</b>, amplified at 20 CODIS STR loci + Amelogenin → matches victim ($1\\text{ in }10^{18}$ random match probability).</div>' },

    { h: 'Semen, Spermatozoa Morphology, Hair & Skeletal Remains',
      b: '<div class="vs-grid">' +
         '<div class="vs-card"><h4>🔍 Semen: Presumptive Screening</h4><ul>' +
         '<li><b>UV / Alternate Light Source (ALS, ~450 nm):</b> Dried semen stains fluoresce bluish-white (non-specific).</li>' +
         '<li><b>Acid Phosphatase (AP / Brentamine Fast Blue B) Test:</b> Human prostatic fluid contains massive concentrations of the enzyme <b>seminal acid phosphatase</b> (400× higher than other body fluids). Reacts with sodium alpha-naphthyl phosphate + Fast Blue B dye to give a rapid (&lt;30 s) <b>deep purple azo colour</b>.</li></ul></div>' +
         '<div class="vs-card"><h4>✅ Semen: Confirmatory Tests</h4><ul>' +
         '<li><b>Microscopic Identification of Spermatozoa:</b> Gold-standard visual confirmation! Stained with <b>Christmas Tree Stain</b> (Nuclear Fast Red stains the <b>sperm head crimson red</b> with a pale pink acrosomal cap; Picroindigocarmine stains the <b>tail &amp; midpiece green-blue</b>) or Baecchi/H&amp;E stain.</li>' +
         '<li><b>PSA (Prostate-Specific Antigen / p30) or Semenogelin:</b> Immunochromatographic strip test — <b>confirms human semen even in azoospermic or vasectomised men</b> where zero sperm cells are present!</li>' +
         '<li><b>Classical Crystal Tests (older/supplementary):</b> <b>Florence test</b> (potassium tri-iodide → brown rhombic crystals of <b>choline periodide</b>) &amp; <b>Barberio test</b> (saturated picric acid → yellow needle-shaped crystals of <b>spermine picrate</b>).</li></ul></div>' +
         '</div>' +
         '<p><b>Morphology of a Human Spermatozoon (Total length ~55–60 µm):</b></p>' +
         '<div class="diagram">[1. Head (~4–5 µm, flattened oval/pear-shaped): Anterior Acrosomal Cap (hyaluronidase/acrosin enzymes to penetrate ovum) + Haploid Nucleus (23 chromosomes: 22 + X or Y)]<br>          │<br>[2. Neck (centriole) &amp; Midpiece (~5–7 µm): Helical sheath packed with Mitochondria generating ATP energy]<br>          │<br>[3. Tail / Flagellum (~45–50 µm): 9+2 microtubule axoneme providing whip-like motility]</div>' +
         '<p><b>Differential DNA Extraction (Gill et al., 1985):</b> In vaginal swabs from sexual assaults, female epithelial cells vastly outnumber sperm. Mild lysis buffer (SDS + Proteinase K) lyses the fragile female epithelial cells first (yielding the <b>F1 female fraction</b>). Because the sperm head nuclear membrane is cross-linked by strong <b>disulphide ($\text{–S–S–}$) bonds</b>, intact sperm heads survive, are pelleted by centrifugation, and are then lysed by adding <b>DTT (Dithiothreitol)</b> — a reducing agent that cleaves disulphide bonds — yielding a clean <b>F2 male sperm DNA fraction</b>!</p>' +
         '<p><b>Forensic Hair Examination:</b> Hair is an appendage of skin composed of keratin protein growing from a follicle in three stages: <b>Anagen</b> (active growth, up to 6 years; pulled hair has ribbon-like follicular tag rich in <b>nuclear DNA</b>), <b>Catagen</b> (transitional, 2–3 weeks), and <b>Telogen</b> (resting/naturally shed phase; dry club-shaped root with little/no nDNA, analysed via <b>mitochondrial DNA</b> in the shaft).<br>' +
         'Three concentric microscopic layers: (1) <b>Cuticle</b> (outer overlapping scales pointing toward tip: <b>imbricate/flattened in humans</b>, coronal/crown-like in bats/rodents, spinous/petal-like in cats/mink); (2) <b>Cortex</b> (bulk of shaft containing melanin pigment granules and cortical fusi); (3) <b>Medulla</b> (central canal: continuous, interrupted, fragmented, or absent; amorphous in humans vs patterned lattice/ladder in animals).<br>' +
         '<b>Medullary Index (MI)</b> = $\\text{Diameter of Medulla} \\div \\text{Total Diameter of Hair Shaft}$: in <b>humans $\\text{MI} < 0.33$</b> (usually $0.10\\text{–}0.25$ or medulla absent); in <b>animals $\\text{MI} > 0.50$</b>.</p>' +
         '<p><b>Human Skeletal System (Forensic Anthropology — 206 adult bones, ~300 at birth):</b></p>' +
         '<ul><li><b>Sex Determination (Reliability order):</b> (1) <b>Pelvis / Innominate bone (~95% accuracy)</b> — female pelvis is wide and shallow for childbirth with a <b>subpubic angle $> 90^\\circ$ (U-shaped)</b>, a <b>wide greater sciatic notch ($> 68^\\circ$)</b>, presence of a ventral arc and preauricular sulcus, and triangular obturator foramen (males have V-shaped subpubic angle $< 70^\\circ$ and narrow sciatic notch); (2) <b>Skull (~85–90%)</b> — males have prominent supraorbital brow ridges, large mastoid processes, rugged nuchal crest, and square chin; (3) Pelvis + Skull together (~98%).</li>' +
         '<li><b>Age Estimation:</b> In sub-adults: <b>dental eruption</b> (deciduous vs permanent teeth; third molar ~18–21 yrs) and <b>epiphyseal union of long bones</b>. In adults: <b>pubic symphysis remodeling</b> (Suchey–Brooks phases), auricular surface of ilium, sternal rib ends, and cranial suture closure.</li>' +
         '<li><b>Stature Estimation:</b> Mathematical regression formulae (<b>Trotter &amp; Gleser</b> or population-specific Indian formulae) applied to maximum length of lower-limb long bones: <b>Femur + Tibia</b> (or <b>Femur alone</b>) gives the smallest standard error.</li></ul>' }
  ],

  questions: [
    { q: 'DNA extracted from a liquid blood sample comes principally from', o: ['Red blood cells', 'White blood cells', 'Platelets', 'Plasma proteins'], a: 1,
      e: '<p><b>Mature human red blood cells have no nucleus</b> and therefore no nuclear DNA. The DNA in a blood sample comes from <b>leucocytes</b>.</p><p>Note that nucleated RBCs <em>are</em> found in birds, reptiles, amphibians and fish — which is one way species origin can be suggested microscopically.</p>' },

    { q: 'A person of blood group AB has', o: ['Anti-A and anti-B antibodies in serum', 'No ABO antibodies in serum', 'Only anti-A antibody', 'No antigens on the red cells'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>Group AB carries <b>both A and B antigens</b> on the red cells and therefore <b>no ABO antibodies</b> in serum — hence "universal recipient".</p><p>Group O is the mirror image: no antigens, both antibodies, universal donor. Option (d) describes group O, the deliberate near-miss.</p>' },

    { q: 'Luminol is used at a crime scene primarily to', o: ['Confirm that a stain is human blood', 'Detect latent or cleaned-up bloodstains by chemiluminescence', 'Determine the ABO group of a stain', 'Fix bloodstains for transport'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>Luminol reacts with the <b>peroxidase activity of haem</b> to emit blue light in darkness, revealing traces diluted to about 1:1,000,000 — including stains someone has attempted to wash away.</p><p>It is strictly <b>presumptive</b>: it gives false positives with bleach, some metals and plant peroxidases, and cannot establish that blood is human. Species requires the <b>precipitin</b> test.</p>' },

    { q: 'The medullary index of human hair is generally', o: ['Less than 0.33', 'Between 0.5 and 0.8', 'Greater than 1.0', 'Exactly 0.5'], a: 0,
      e: '<p>Medullary index = medulla diameter ÷ total hair diameter. It is <b>below 0.33 in humans</b> and typically <b>above 0.5 in animals</b>, making it a quick and reliable human-vs-animal discriminator.</p><p>Human medullae are also frequently fragmented or absent altogether, whereas animal medullae are usually continuous and well defined.</p>' },

    { q: 'Mitochondrial DNA is particularly valuable in forensic casework because it is', o: ['Unique to each individual', 'Present in high copy number and maternally inherited', 'Found only in the cell nucleus', 'More discriminating than STR typing'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>High copy number means mtDNA survives in degraded material where nuclear DNA has been destroyed — old bone, teeth, and <b>rootless hair shafts</b>.</p><p>Its limitation is the flip side of maternal inheritance: <b>all maternal relatives share the same sequence</b>, so it cannot individualise. Options (a) and (d) both invert this.</p>' },

    { q: 'The confirmatory test for the presence of semen in a stain is', o: ['The acid phosphatase test', 'Microscopic identification of spermatozoa', 'The Kastle-Meyer test', 'The precipitin test'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>Acid phosphatase is <b>presumptive</b> only. Confirmation requires <b>visualising spermatozoa</b>, or detecting <b>PSA (p30)</b> — essential where the donor is azoospermic or vasectomised.</p><p>Kastle-Meyer is a presumptive blood test and precipitin determines species — both belong to a different analyte, which is what the question checks.</p>' },

    { q: 'In a DNA sample, 30% of the bases are adenine. The percentage of guanine is', o: ['30%', '20%', '40%', '70%'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>By <b>Chargaff\'s rule</b>, A = T, so T is also 30% and A+T = 60%. The remaining <b>40%</b> is G+C, split equally since G = C — hence <b>20% guanine</b>.</p><p>Note this holds only for double-stranded DNA. Single-stranded DNA and RNA do not obey Chargaff\'s rule, which is a common follow-up trap.</p>' },

    { q: 'A secretor is a person who', o: ['Has blood group O', 'Secretes ABO blood group antigens into body fluids such as saliva and semen', 'Produces excess sweat', 'Lacks the Rh antigen'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>About <b>80%</b> of people are secretors, releasing their ABO antigens into saliva, semen, sweat and vaginal secretions. Detection is by <b>absorption-inhibition</b>.</p><p>Before DNA typing this permitted blood grouping from a stain containing no blood at all — historically important, and the reason the syllabus still names it.</p>' },

    { q: 'Which skeletal element is most reliable for determining the sex of unidentified remains?', o: ['Femur', 'Skull', 'Pelvis', 'Sternum'], a: 2,
      e: '<p>The <b>pelvis</b> is most reliable, because it is adapted for childbirth in females — a wider sciatic notch, a subpubic angle exceeding 90°, and a broader, shallower shape overall.</p><p>The skull is second best. The femur is used chiefly for <b>stature</b> estimation via Trotter and Gleser regression — a different question in the biological profile.</p>' },

    { q: 'Which microcrystal test confirms the presence of blood by heating the stain with pyridine, glucose and sodium hydroxide to produce salmon-pink feathery crystals of haemochromogen?', o: ['Teichmann test', 'Takayama test', 'Florence test', 'Barberio test'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>The <b>Takayama test</b> uses pyridine, NaOH and glucose to form salmon-pink feathery crystals of <b>pyridine ferroprotoporphyrin (haemochromogen)</b>. The <b>Teichmann test</b> uses glacial acetic acid and a halide salt (NaCl) to yield brown rhombic crystals of haemin. Florence and Barberio are crystal tests for <em>semen</em>.</p>' },

    { q: 'Once a stain has been confirmed as blood by crystal tests, which immunological test is classically performed next to determine whether the blood is of HUMAN origin (species identification)?', o: ['Kastle-Meyer test', 'Precipitin test (using anti-human globulin antiserum)', 'Acid phosphatase test', 'Luminol spray'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>The <b>Precipitin test</b> (Uhlenhuth ring test, Ouchterlony gel diffusion, or crossover electrophoresis) reacts human protein antigens extracted from the stain with <b>anti-human serum</b> raised in rabbits; formation of a white precipitin line confirms that the blood is of <b>human</b> origin rather than animal.</p>' },

    { q: 'In a sexual assault case involving a vasectomised or azoospermic perpetrator (whose ejaculate contains no spermatozoa), which protein marker is used to confirm the presence of human semen?', o: ['Haemoglobin', 'Prostate-Specific Antigen (PSA / p30)', 'Amylase', 'Keratin'], a: 1,
      e: '<p><b>Prostate-Specific Antigen (PSA, also called p30)</b> is a glycoprotein secreted into seminal plasma by the prostate gland independently of sperm production in the testes. Therefore, an immunochromatographic PSA (or Semenogelin) test confirms human semen even when microscopic examination shows zero spermatozoa.</p>' },

    { q: 'During differential extraction of a sexual assault vaginal swab, which chemical reducing agent is added in the second step to cleave the disulphide bonds of the sperm head membrane and release male DNA?', o: ['Sodium fluoride', 'Dithiothreitol (DTT)', 'Phenolphthalein', 'Hydrogen peroxide'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>Sperm nuclear membranes are rich in disulphide ($\text{–S–S–}$) cross-links, allowing them to survive initial mild SDS/Proteinase K lysis that breaks open female epithelial cells. Adding <b>Dithiothreitol (DTT)</b> reduces those disulphide bonds and lyses the pelleted sperm heads to yield an uncontaminated male DNA profile.</p>' },

    { q: 'Which growth phase of hair yields the highest success rate for routine nuclear STR DNA profiling because the root typically carries an intact follicular sheath of nucleated cells?', o: ['Telogen (resting / naturally shed club hair)', 'Anagen (actively growing hair pulled forcibly)', 'Catagen (regressing phase)', 'Cuticular phase'], a: 1,
      e: '<p><b>Anagen</b> is the active growth phase (~85% of scalp hairs at any time). When an anagen hair is forcibly pulled during a struggle, it brings along a translucent <b>follicular tag</b> rich in nucleated cells ideal for autosomal STR profiling. Naturally shed <b>telogen</b> hairs have dry keratinised club roots and typically require <b>mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA)</b> sequencing of the shaft.</p>' },

    { q: 'In a human spermatozoon, the midpiece is densely packed with which organelle to supply ATP energy for flagellar motility?', o: ['Ribosomes', 'Mitochondria', 'Lysosomes', 'Golgi bodies'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>The <b>midpiece</b> of the spermatozoon contains a helical sheath of <b>mitochondria</b> that generate the ATP required for the whip-like movement of the tail (flagellum). The <b>head</b> contains the haploid nucleus and the enzyme-filled <b>acrosome cap</b>.</p>' }
  ]
});

window.MPSC.units.push({
  id: 'p3gf6',
  paper: 'Paper III — General Forensic',
  title: 'VI. Forensic Toxicology',
  marks: 10,
  syllabus: 'Forensic Toxicology - introduction, concept and significance. Definition and classification of poisons. Definition and classification of liquors based on Origin (Indian made Foreign liquor, country made liquor and illicit liquors). Fermented and distilled methods. Characteristics of Beer, Wine and whiskey. Analysis of beverages - alcoholic and non alcoholic. Viscera and its preservation.',

  notes: [
    { h: 'Forensic Toxicology & Classification of Poisons',
      b: '<p><b>Father of Toxicology:</b> <b>Paracelsus</b> (1493–1541) — <em>"All things are poison and nothing is without poison; the dose alone makes a thing not a poison."</em> <b>Father of Modern Forensic Toxicology:</b> <b>Mathieu Orfila</b> (1814, perfected the Marsh test to detect arsenic in human viscera/tissues, proving systemic absorption).<br>' +
         '<b>LD50 (Median Lethal Dose):</b> The dose (mg/kg body weight) that kills 50% of a test animal population. <b>The lower the LD50, the more potent/deadly the poison!</b></p>' +
         '<table><tr><th>Class of Poison</th><th>Sub-classes &amp; Classic Examples</th><th>Forensic &amp; Autopsy Hallmarks</th></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>1. Corrosives</b> (Local tissue destruction)</td><td><b>Strong Acids:</b> H₂SO₄, HNO₃, HCl, Oxalic acid, Carbolic acid (Phenol)<br><b>Strong Alkalis:</b> NaOH, KOH, Ammonia</td><td>H₂SO₄ chars stomach black ("blotting-paper stomach"); HNO₃ stains yellow; Carbolic acid gives green/smoky carboluria &amp; white leathery stomach; Alkalis cause deep liquefactive necrosis.</td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>2. Irritants</b> (Severe GI pain, vomiting, purging)</td><td><b>Inorganic (Metallic):</b> Arsenic, Mercury, Lead, Thallium, Copper<br><b>Inorganic (Non-metallic):</b> Phosphorus, Iodine<br><b>Organic (Plant):</b> Ricin (<em>Ricinus communis</em> / castor), Abrin (<em>Abrus precatorius</em> / ratti), Croton oil, Calotropis<br><b>Organic (Animal):</b> Snake venoms (Elapid = neurotoxic; Viper = haemotoxic), Cantharides<br><b>Mechanical:</b> Powdered glass, diamond dust, chopped hair</td><td><b>Arsenic:</b> Cholera-like "rice-water stools", garlic breath, <b>Mees\' lines</b> on fingernails, raindrop pigmentation, delays putrefaction; tested by <b>Marsh test / Reinsch test / Gutzeit test</b>.<br><b>Lead (Plumbism):</b> Burtonian blue line on gums, wrist-drop, basophilic stippling of RBCs.<br><b>Mercury:</b> Hatter\'s shakes (tremor), erethism.<br><b>Thallium:</b> Painful neuropathy + complete <b>alopecia (hair loss)</b>.<br><b>Yellow Phosphorus:</b> Garlic odour, luminous vomit/faeces in the dark ("smoking stool syndrome"), fatty liver.</td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>3. Neurotics (Act on CNS / Spine / Nerves)</b></td><td><b>Cerebral — Somniferous (Opioids):</b> Opium, Morphine, Heroin<br><b>Cerebral — Inebriant:</b> Ethanol, Methanol, Barbiturates, Benzodiazepines, Chloroform<br><b>Cerebral — Deliriant:</b> <b>Datura</b> (<em>Datura fastuosa</em> — atropine/hyoscine/scopolamine; "roadside/train poison"), Cannabis, Cocaine<br><b>Spinal:</b> <b>Strychnine</b> (<em>Strychnos nux-vomica</em> / Kuchila seeds)<br><b>Peripheral:</b> Curare, Coniine (Hemlock)</td><td><b>Opioids:</b> Coma + <b>pinpoint pupils</b> + respiratory depression (antidote: <b>Naloxone</b>).<br><b>Datura:</b> "Blind as a bat, mad as a hatter, red as a beet, hot as a hare, dry as a bone" (dilated pupils, delirium, muttering, picking at imaginary threads — carphologia); antidote: <b>Physostigmine</b>.<br><b>Strychnine:</b> Blocks inhibitory glycine receptors in spinal Renshaw cells → <b>opisthotonos</b> (body arched on head and heels) + <b>risus sardonicus</b> with a <b>fully conscious mind</b>!</td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>4. Cardiac Poisons</b></td><td><b>Aconite</b> ("Queen of Poisons" / Monkshood — sweetish taste + tingling/numbness of tongue), <b>Yellow/White Oleander</b> (Kaner / <em>Cerbera thevetia</em>), Digitalis, Tobacco (Nicotine)</td><td>Arrhythmias, heart block, hippus (alternate contraction/dilation of pupil in aconite).</td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>5. Asphyxiants (Irrespirable Gases)</b></td><td><b>Carbon Monoxide (CO)</b>, <b>Hydrogen Cyanide (HCN)</b>, Hydrogen Sulphide (H₂S — rotten egg smell + sewer gas), CO₂</td><td><b>CO:</b> 200–250× affinity for Hb → Carboxyhaemoglobin → <b>cherry-red</b> post-mortem lividity.<br><b>Cyanide:</b> Inhibits cytochrome c oxidase (cellular histotoxic hypoxia) → <b>bright brick-red lividity</b> + <b>bitter almond odour</b>; antidote: <b>Amyl/Sodium nitrite + Sodium thiosulphate</b> (or Hydroxocobalamin).</td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>6. Agrochemical Insecticides</b></td><td><b>Organophosphates (OP):</b> Parathion, Malathion, Chlorpyrifos<br><b>Carbamates:</b> Carbaryl, Propoxur<br><b>Organochlorines:</b> DDT, Endrin, Endosulfan<br><b>Aluminium Phosphide (Celphos):</b> Releases lethal <b>Phosphine ($\\text{PH}_3$)</b> gas</td><td><b>Organophosphates:</b> Irreversibly inhibit <b>Acetylcholinesterase (AChE)</b> → acetylcholine overload (SLUDGE syndrome: salivation, lacrimation, urination, defecation, pinpoint miosis, bronchospasm, kerosene/garlic odour). Antidote: <b>Atropine + Pralidoxime (2-PAM)</b>.</td></tr></table>' },

    { h: 'Viscera Collection, Packaging & Preservatives',
      b: '<p>In every suspected fatal poisoning autopsy, viscera are collected in clean, wide-mouthed glass jars (filling only two-thirds full to allow room for preservative and expansion) and sent to the Forensic Science Laboratory (FSL) under unbroken seal:</p>' +
         '<table><tr><th>Bottle / Container</th><th>Viscera / Specimen Collected</th><th>Quantity &amp; Forensic Purpose</th></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>Bottle No. 1 (Alimentary Tract)</b></td><td><b>Stomach (tied at both ends) + entire stomach contents</b> + upper <b>30 cm of small intestine</b> with contents</td><td>Represents the <b>unabsorbed fraction</b> of ingested poison; shows mucosal corrosion, irritation or undissolved tablets/datura seeds.</td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>Bottle No. 2 (Solid Organs of Metabolism/Excretion)</b></td><td><b>Liver (~500 g, including gallbladder)</b> + <b>Half of each kidney</b> + half of spleen</td><td>Represents the <b>absorbed and metabolised fraction</b>. Liver is the primary site of detoxification and concentrates alkaloids/metals; kidney is the primary organ of excretion.</td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>Bottle No. 3 (Body Fluids)</b></td><td><b>Blood (~10–30 mL from femoral/subclavian vein)</b> + <b>Urine (~30–50 mL)</b></td><td>Femoral blood gives accurate quantitative toxic blood level at time of death (avoids post-mortem redistribution from stomach into heart blood).</td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>Bottle No. 4 (Control Sample — MANDATORY)</b></td><td><b>100 mL of the pure preservative used</b> (taken from the same stock bottle)</td><td>Rules out defence allegations that the poison (e.g., arsenic or alcohol) was already present as an impurity in the hospital\'s preservative!</td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>Special Additional Specimens</b></td><td>• <b>Brain (~200–300 g):</b> Volatile/lipophilic poisons (chloroform, cyanide, alcohol, barbiturates, OP)<br>• <b>Lungs (tied at bronchus):</b> Inhaled gaseous poisons (CO, HCN, anaesthetic gases)<br>• <b>Plucked scalp hair, nails &amp; long bone (femur):</b> <b>Chronic Arsenic / Heavy Metal poisoning</b> (deposits in sulphydryl-rich keratin)<br>• <b>Vitreous Humour of Eye:</b> Best protected fluid against putrefaction for post-mortem alcohol/electrolytes</td><td>Collected in separate labelled containers whenever specific toxidromes are suspected.</td></tr></table>' +
         '<div class="example"><b>🔬 Worked Example — Viscera Preservative Selection Matrix (The Golden Rules):</b><br>' +
         'Choosing the wrong preservative destroys the case in court. Memorise this exact decision table:<br>' +
         '1. <b>Routine Viscera (General Poisoning / Insecticides / Alkaloids / Heavy Metals):</b> Use <b>Saturated Solution of Common Salt (Sodium Chloride, NaCl)</b> — except when corrosive sublimate/mercury or sodium chloride poisoning itself is suspected.<br>' +
         '2. <b>Rectified Spirit (90–95% Ethanol):</b> Can be used for general non-alcoholic poisons, <b>EXCEPT</b> you must <b>NEVER use Rectified Spirit in cases of suspected Alcohol, Methanol, Chloral Hydrate, Chloroform, Ether, Phenol (Carbolic acid), Phosphorus, or Paralaldehyde poisoning</b> (because spirit IS alcohol or dissolves/reacts with them!).<br>' +
         '3. <b>Suspected Alcohol / Methanol / Drunken Driving Blood Sample:</b> Use <b>Sodium Fluoride (NaF, 10 mg/mL) + Potassium Oxalate (3 mg/mL)</b> in a grey-top tube. <b>Sodium fluoride is an enzyme inhibitor (inhibits enolase in glycolysis)</b> that stops bacteria/yeast from fermenting blood glucose into post-mortem ethanol, while <b>potassium oxalate</b> prevents clotting!<br>' +
         '4. <b>Suspected Heavy Metals / Corrosive Acids &amp; Alkalis (where NaCl contains trace metal impurities):</b> Use <b>Rectified Spirit</b> (or send deep-frozen on dry ice without chemical preservative).<br>' +
         '5. <b>Formalin (Formaldehyde):</b> <b>NEVER use Formalin for toxicological viscera!</b> Formalin fixes protein matrices and destroys alkaloids, cyanide and volatile poisons (reserved strictly for small histopathology tissue blocks).</div>' },

    { h: 'Liquors: Classification, Manufacture, Proof Spirit & Methanol Antidote',
      b: '<p><b>Classification of Alcoholic Beverages by Origin (Excise &amp; Forensic):</b></p>' +
         '<ul><li><b>IMFL (Indian Made Foreign Liquor):</b> Distilled spirits manufactured in licensed Indian distilleries following Western formulations and BIS standards: <b>Whisky, Rum, Brandy, Gin, Vodka</b> (typically <b>42.8% v/v ethanol = 75° Proof</b>).</li>' +
         '<li><b>Country Liquor (Desi Sharab / Arrack / Fenny / Toddy):</b> Licensed, locally produced low-cost fermented or distilled beverages made from regional raw materials (molasses, mahua flowers, palm sap, cashew apple, rice).</li>' +
         '<li><b>Illicit Liquor (Hooch / Spurious Liquor / Lattha):</b> Unlicensed, illegally brewed/distilled alcohol. Often adulterated with industrial methylated spirit, urea, ammonium chloride, chloral hydrate, or organophosphates to increase "kick", and heavily contaminated with deadly <b>Methanol (Methyl alcohol / Wood spirit, $\\text{CH}_3\\text{OH}$)</b> from poor distillation temperature control or denatured spirit addition.</li></ul>' +
         '<p><b>Fermented vs Distilled Beverages:</b></p>' +
         '<ul><li><b>Fermentation:</b> Anaerobic conversion of hexose sugars into ethanol and carbon dioxide by the zymase enzyme complex of yeast (<em>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</em>): $\\text{C}_6\\text{H}_{12}\\text{O}_6 \\xrightarrow{\\text{yeast}} 2\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_5\\text{OH} + 2\\text{CO}_2$. Because ethanol denatures yeast membranes above <b>14–15% ABV</b>, natural fermentation alone can NEVER exceed ~15% alcohol.</li>' +
         '<li><b>Distillation:</b> Heating the fermented wash in a still so ethanol (boiling point <b>78.3 °C</b>) vaporises ahead of water (boiling point <b>100 °C</b>) and condenses at 40–95% ABV. (At 95.6% ethanol + 4.4% water, an azeotrope forms called <b>Rectified Spirit</b>; removing the last 4.4% water chemically yields 99.9% <b>Absolute Alcohol</b>; adding <b>methanol + pyridine + caoutchoucine</b> makes poisonous <b>Denatured / Methylated Spirit</b> unfit for drinking.)</li></ul>' +
         '<table><tr><th>Beverage</th><th>Raw Material</th><th>Process</th><th>Typical Alcohol (% ABV)</th></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>Beer / Lager / Ale</b></td><td>Malted barley grain + <b>Hops</b> (for bitterness) + water + yeast</td><td><b>Fermented</b> (undistilled)</td><td>4–8% v/v</td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>Wine</b></td><td>Crushed grapes / fruit juice</td><td><b>Fermented</b> (undistilled; Fortified wines like Port/Sherry add brandy to reach 18–20%)</td><td>9–15% v/v</td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>Whisky</b></td><td>Fermented grain mash (barley, corn, rye, wheat)</td><td><b>Distilled</b> &amp; aged in charred oak casks</td><td>40–43% v/v (75° Proof in India)</td></tr>' +
         '<tr><td><b>Rum / Brandy / Vodka</b></td><td>Sugarcane molasses (Rum); Grapes (Brandy); Grain/potatoes (Vodka)</td><td><b>Distilled</b></td><td>40–43% v/v</td></tr></table>' +
         '<div class="clarity"><b>⚡ Concept Clarity — British/Indian "Proof Spirit" vs American Proof:</b><ul>' +
         '<li><b>British / Indian System:</b> <b>100° Proof = 57.1% v/v ethanol</b> (49.28% by weight). Thus $1\\%\\text{ v/v} \\approx 1.75^\\circ\\text{ Proof}$. Standard Indian Whisky at <b>42.8% v/v is $42.8 \\times 1.75 =$ 75° Proof (i.e., 25° Under Proof / UP)</b>.</li>' +
         '<li><b>US System:</b> Proof is simply <b>$2 \\times \\text{ABV}$</b> (so 100 US Proof = 50% v/v). Always assume the <b>57.1% British/Indian definition</b> in Indian forensic exams!</li></ul></div>' +
         '<div class="analogy"><b>💡 Analogy — How Ethanol Acts as the Antidote for Methanol (Hooch) Poisoning:</b> Methanol ($\\text{CH}_3\\text{OH}$) itself only makes a person mildly drowsy. The tragedy happens when the liver enzyme <b>Alcohol Dehydrogenase (ADH)</b> grabs methanol and converts it into <b>Formaldehyde</b>, and Aldehyde Dehydrogenase turns that into <b>Formic Acid</b> — which attacks the retina and optic nerve ("snowstorm vision" → permanent blindness) and causes lethal metabolic acidosis. Giving the patient <b>Ethanol (or Fomepizole)</b> is like playing <b>musical chairs where Ethanol has 10 to 20 times higher affinity for the ADH enzyme chair than Methanol</b>: Ethanol hogs all the ADH enzyme seats so Methanol cannot sit down and get converted into toxic formic acid, allowing the kidneys and lungs to excrete the unchanged methanol safely!</div>' +
         '<p><b>Forensic Analysis of Alcoholic &amp; Non-Alcoholic Beverages:</b></p>' +
         '<ul><li><b>Colour / Presumptive Chemical Tests:</b><ul>' +
         '<li><b>Potassium Dichromate ($\\text{K}_2\\text{Cr}_2\\text{O}_7$) + conc. $\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4$ (Kozelka &amp; Hine / Cavett / Breathalyser reaction):</b> Orange dichromate ($\text{Cr}^{6+}$) is reduced by ethanol/methanol to <b>green Chromium(III) sulphate ($\text{Cr}^{3+}$)</b>.</li>' +
         '<li><b>Iodoform Test ($\text{I}_2 + \text{NaOH}$):</b> Ethanol gives yellow hexagonal crystals of <b>iodoform ($\text{CHI}_3$)</b> with an antiseptic smell (pure methanol does NOT give iodoform!).</li>' +
         '<li><b>Schiff\'s Reagent / Chromotropic Acid Test (Specific for Methanol):</b> Oxidise sample gently with $\\text{KMnO}_4 + \\text{H}_3\\text{PO}_4$ (converting methanol to formaldehyde), decolorise with sodium bisulphite, then add <b>Chromotropic acid + conc. $\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4$ → deep violet-purple colour</b> confirms <b>Methanol</b>!</li></ul></li>' +
         '<li><b>Instrumental Gold Standard:</b> <b>Headspace Gas Chromatography with Flame Ionisation Detector (HS-GC-FID)</b> or <b>GC-MS</b> (using n-propanol or t-butanol as internal standard) simultaneously separates and quantifies ethanol, methanol, acetaldehyde, and fusel oils (amyl/isoamyl/isobutyl alcohols). Specific gravity by pycnometer and alcoholic strength by distillation + hydrometer verify BIS standards. Under Section 185 of the Motor Vehicles Act in India, the legal blood alcohol concentration (BAC) limit for driving is <b>30 mg per 100 mL of blood (0.03%)</b>.</li></ul>' }
  ],

  questions: [
    { q: 'When alcohol poisoning is suspected, the correct preservative for a blood sample is', o: ['Rectified spirit', 'Saturated saline', 'Sodium fluoride with potassium oxalate', 'Formalin'], a: 2,
      e: '<p><b>Sodium fluoride</b> inhibits the microbial fermentation that would otherwise <em>generate</em> alcohol in the sample after collection, and <b>potassium oxalate</b> acts as anticoagulant.</p><p><b>Rectified spirit must never be used</b> where alcohol is in question — it is itself alcohol and would destroy the finding. That trap is the entire point of the question.</p>' },

    { q: 'Methanol poisoning causes blindness because methanol is metabolised to', o: ['Acetaldehyde and acetic acid', 'Formaldehyde and formic acid', 'Ethanol and water', 'Oxalic acid only'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>Alcohol dehydrogenase converts methanol to <b>formaldehyde</b> and then <b>formic acid</b>, which damages the optic nerve and produces severe metabolic acidosis.</p><p>The antidote is <b>ethanol or fomepizole</b>: both compete for alcohol dehydrogenase, so methanol is excreted unchanged. Acetaldehyde and acetic acid (option a) are the <em>ethanol</em> metabolites.</p>' },

    { q: 'Cherry-red post-mortem lividity is characteristic of poisoning by', o: ['Cyanide', 'Carbon monoxide', 'Arsenic', 'Organophosphate'], a: 1,
      e: '<p><b>Carbon monoxide</b> forms carboxyhaemoglobin, which is bright cherry-red. CO binds haemoglobin roughly 200–250 times more avidly than oxygen.</p><p><b>Cyanide</b> gives a <em>brick-red</em> lividity with a bitter almond odour — the intended near-miss. Arsenic gives no characteristic lividity colour.</p>' },

    { q: 'Fermentation alone cannot produce a beverage stronger than about', o: ['5% alcohol', '15% alcohol', '40% alcohol', '57% alcohol'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>Yeast is killed by its own ethanol at roughly <b>14–15% ABV</b>, which caps fermented drinks such as wine and beer.</p><p>Exceeding that requires <b>distillation</b>, exploiting the boiling point difference between ethanol (78.3 °C) and water. This is precisely why whisky, rum and brandy are classed as distilled rather than fermented beverages.</p>' },

    { q: 'In a routine viscera collection, the approximate quantity of liver preserved is', o: ['50 g', '100 g', '500 g', '1500 g'], a: 2,
      e: '<p>About <b>500 g of liver</b> is taken, because the liver is the chief organ of metabolism and detoxification and therefore concentrates most poisons.</p><p>The standard set also includes stomach with contents, upper small intestine with contents, half of each kidney, blood and urine — with hair and nails added where <b>chronic arsenic</b> is suspected.</p>' },

    { q: 'Mees\' lines on the nails are a classic sign of chronic poisoning by', o: ['Lead', 'Arsenic', 'Mercury', 'Thallium'], a: 1,
      e: '<p><b>Mees\' lines</b> — transverse white bands across the nails — are classically associated with <b>arsenic</b>. Chronic exposure also gives "rice water" stools, a garlic odour and peripheral neuropathy.</p><p>Because arsenic is deposited in <b>keratin</b>, hair and nails retain it long after death and can even give a rough exposure timeline along the length of a hair.</p>' },

    { q: 'In the Indian system, 100° proof spirit corresponds to an alcohol content of approximately', o: ['50.0% ABV', '57.1% ABV', '75.0% ABV', '100% ABV'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>In the British/Indian system, <b>100° proof = 57.1% ABV</b>. The historic definition is the weakest spirit that will still permit gunpowder soaked in it to ignite.</p><p>Do not confuse this with the American system, where proof is simply twice the ABV, making 100 proof = 50% — which is option (a), the deliberate trap.</p>' },

    { q: 'Strychnine poisoning characteristically produces', o: ['Pinpoint pupils and excessive secretions', 'Violent convulsions with the victim remaining conscious', 'Cherry-red lividity', 'Rice water stools'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>Strychnine blocks inhibitory glycine receptors in the spinal cord, producing violent generalised convulsions with <b>opisthotonos</b> and <b>risus sardonicus</b> — while <b>consciousness is retained</b>, which is its grim hallmark.</p><p>Option (a) is organophosphate poisoning, (c) carbon monoxide and (d) arsenic. Each option is a genuine signature for a different poison, which is what makes this a good discriminator.</p>' },

    { q: 'Why is Ethanol (or Fomepizole) administered intravenously as the specific antidote in acute Methanol (spurious hooch) poisoning?', o: ['It neutralises formic acid in the stomach by acid-base titration', 'It competitively inhibits the enzyme Alcohol Dehydrogenase (ADH), preventing methanol from being metabolised into toxic formaldehyde and formic acid', 'It precipitates methanol out of the blood as a solid salt', 'It stimulates the optic nerve directly'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>Ethanol has a <b>10–20× higher affinity for Alcohol Dehydrogenase (ADH)</b> than methanol (and Fomepizole has ~8,000× higher affinity). By competitively occupying ADH, it blocks the conversion of methanol into <b>formaldehyde and formic acid</b>, giving the kidneys time to excrete unchanged methanol safely.</p>' },

    { q: 'In a case of Organophosphate (e.g., parathion, chlorpyrifos) insecticide poisoning, the toxic mechanism and the specific combined antidote are respectively', o: ['Glycine receptor blockade; treated with Naloxone', 'Irreversible inhibition of Acetylcholinesterase (AChE); treated with Atropine + Pralidoxime (2-PAM)', 'Cytochrome oxidase inhibition; treated with Sodium Nitrite + Sodium Thiosulphate', 'Haemoglobin binding; treated with 100% hyperbaric oxygen'], a: 1,
      e: '<p><b>Organophosphates</b> phosphorylate and inhibit <b>acetylcholinesterase (AChE)</b>, causing massive acetylcholine accumulation (pinpoint pupils/miosis, bronchorrhoea, salivation, bradycardia). <b>Atropine</b> blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, while <b>Pralidoxime (2-PAM / oxime)</b> reactivates the phosphorylated AChE enzyme before "ageing" occurs.</p>' },

    { q: 'During an autopsy of a suspected poisoning case, why is it mandatory for the medical officer to send a separate sealed bottle containing a sample of the pure preservative (e.g. saturated saline) to the Forensic Science Laboratory?', o: ['To allow the chemist to top up the viscera jars if they leak', 'To serve as a negative control proving that any detected poison (such as arsenic or heavy metal) was not already present as a contaminant in the preservative itself', 'To test the specific gravity of the glass bottle', 'To preserve the chain-of-custody paper label'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>Commercial common salt or spirit can occasionally contain trace impurities (including arsenic or heavy metals). Analysing a <b>control sample of the preservative alone</b> is essential to defeat the standard defence argument that the poison found in the viscera came from contaminated hospital preservative rather than ante-mortem ingestion.</p>' },

    { q: 'Which colour reaction is specifically used in forensic toxicology to detect Methanol adulteration in illicit liquor after oxidising the methanol to formaldehyde with potassium permanganate and phosphoric acid?', o: ['Chromotropic acid test (yielding a deep violet-purple colour)', 'Marquis test', 'Kastle-Meyer test', 'Duquenois-Levine test'], a: 0,
      e: '<p>In the <b>Chromotropic acid test</b> (and Schiff\'s reagent test), methanol is selectively oxidised to <b>formaldehyde</b> using acid permanganate, excess permanganate is decolourised with sodium bisulphite, and heating with <b>chromotropic acid in concentrated $\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4$</b> produces a diagnostic <b>violet-purple colour</b>. Ethanol oxidises to acetaldehyde, which does not give the violet colour with chromotropic acid.</p>' },

    { q: 'Which classical chemical test is specifically used to detect Arsenic in biological tissues/viscera by reducing arsenic compounds with nascent hydrogen (zinc + H₂SO₄) to arsine gas (AsH₃), which decomposes into a metallic arsenic mirror?', o: ['Marsh test (and Gutzeit / Reinsch tests)', 'Walker test', 'Florence test', 'Scott test'], a: 0,
      e: '<p>The <b>Marsh test</b> (developed by James Marsh in 1836), along with the <b>Gutzeit test</b> and <b>Reinsch test</b> (depositing arsenic onto bright copper foil in HCl), is the classic forensic test for <b>arsenic</b>. Today, trace quantification is performed by <b>ICP-MS or AAS (Hydride Generation Atomic Absorption Spectrometry)</b>.</p>' },

    { q: 'Under Section 185 of the Indian Motor Vehicles Act, what is the statutory Blood Alcohol Concentration (BAC) limit beyond which driving a motor vehicle is an offence in India?', o: ['10 mg of alcohol per 100 mL of blood (0.01%)', '30 mg of alcohol per 100 mL of blood (0.03%)', '80 mg of alcohol per 100 mL of blood (0.08%)', '100 mg of alcohol per 100 mL of blood (0.10%)'], a: 1,
      e: '<p>In India, Section 185 of the Motor Vehicles Act sets the legal limit at <b>30 mg of alcohol per 100 mL of blood (0.03% w/v)</b>, detected initially by a fuel-cell/IR breath analyser and confirmed by headspace GC analysis of blood preserved with sodium fluoride and potassium oxalate.</p>' },

    { q: 'Which plant poison, commonly called the "roadside poison" or "stupefying train poison" in India, contains tropane alkaloids (atropine, hyoscyamine, scopolamine) that cause dry mouth, flushed skin, dilated pupils and muttering delirium with amnesia?', o: ['Strychnos nux-vomica (Kuchila)', 'Datura fastuosa (Dhatura)', 'Aconitum napellus (Mitha zahar)', 'Papaver somniferum (Opium)'], a: 1,
      e: '<p><b>Datura</b> crushed seeds (which resemble chilly seeds, though kidney-shaped with a double-ridged margin) are notoriously mixed into tea, sweets or prasad given to railway/bus passengers to induce anticholinergic delirium and anterograde amnesia so their belongings can be robbed.</p>' }
  ]
});
